<header class="lesson">
  <div class="crumb">Lesson 0004 &middot; cpp_games / docs / learning</div>
  <h1>FPGAs, calling them from C++, and running LLMs on them</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: your <code class="inline">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs</code> already uses a sidecar for inference. Researchers are now putting that same inference workload on FPGAs, and getting 1.1&ndash;3.3&times; the speed of a GPU at 3&ndash;6&times; less energy. This lesson is the conceptual bridge from "Ollama on a GPU" to "what if Wren's brain were a programmable chip instead?"</p>
</header>

<h2>1. What an FPGA actually is</h2>

<p>An <b>FPGA</b> (Field-Programmable Gate Array) is a chip whose <em>wiring</em> is programmable. A CPU has a fixed circuit that fetches instructions and runs them. An FPGA ships with a sea of generic logic blocks and wires, and at boot you load a configuration that <em>becomes</em> a specific custom circuit. You're not running software on hardware &mdash; you're describing hardware that the chip then is.</p>

<div class="chip-diagram">
   inside an FPGA, every cell is one of these:

   +----+----+----+----+        LUT  = a tiny 4- or 6-input lookup table
   |LUT |LUT |LUT |LUT |              that you fill in to implement
   |----+ FF +----+ FF |              any boolean function
   |LUT |    |LUT |    |        FF   = a flip-flop (one bit of state)
   +----+----+----+----+        DSP  = a dedicated multiplier-accumulator
   |LUT |LUT |DSP |LUT |        BRAM = a small on-chip RAM block
   |----+ FF |    | FF |
   |LUT |    |    |    |        Routing fabric (the lines between)
   +----+----+----+----+        is itself programmable.
   |BRAM|BRAM|LUT |LUT |
   |    |    |----+ FF |        You "program" an FPGA by deciding which
   |    |    |LUT |    |        cells light up and how they're wired
   +----+----+----+----+        together. The result is a circuit.</div>

<p>You describe what you want using a <b>hardware description language</b> &mdash; Verilog or VHDL &mdash; or with <b>High-Level Synthesis (HLS)</b>, where you write C/C++ and a compiler produces the HDL for you. A synthesizer reduces your description to LUTs/FFs/DSPs, a placer assigns them to physical cells, a router connects them, and the final output is a <b>bitstream</b>: a file that, when loaded into the chip, rewires it to be the circuit you described.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <h3>The one-sentence mental model</h3>
  <p>An FPGA is a chip where you specify the schematic at boot time, then run signals through it &mdash; not instructions through a fixed CPU.</p>
</div>

<h2>2. FPGA vs. CPU vs. GPU</h2>

<p>All three are silicon. They diverge on <em>what they're optimized to do</em>.</p>

<div class="arch-row">
  <div class="arch-card cpu">
    <h4>CPU (your game loop)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Few (4&ndash;16) very smart cores</li>
      <li>Branch prediction, deep caches, out-of-order execution</li>
      <li>Optimized for <b>latency</b> on serial code with unpredictable control flow</li>
      <li>Programmed in C++/Rust/etc.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="arch-card gpu">
    <h4>GPU (Ollama, today)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Thousands of small cores, ganged into "warps"</li>
      <li>Massive memory bandwidth, simple cache</li>
      <li>Optimized for <b>throughput</b> on regular parallel math (matrices, pixels)</li>
      <li>Programmed in CUDA/Vulkan/OpenCL</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="arch-card fpga">
    <h4>FPGA</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Sea of LUTs/FFs/DSPs wired to your spec</li>
      <li>You build a deep custom pipeline; throughput = 1 result per clock</li>
      <li>Optimized for <b>determinism + efficiency</b> on a single fixed workload</li>
      <li>Programmed in Verilog/VHDL/HLS</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>CPU</th><th>GPU</th><th>FPGA</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>"What's it good at?"</td><td>Irregular code, branches, single-threaded latency</td><td>Lots of identical math in parallel</td><td>One specific dataflow done with minimal energy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"How do I program it?"</td><td>Write code, compile, run</td><td>Write a kernel, dispatch from host</td><td>Describe a circuit, synthesize, load bitstream</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Time from idea to running"</td><td>Seconds (compile)</td><td>Seconds (compile)</td><td>Hours (synth + place &amp; route)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Change behaviour at runtime?"</td><td>Trivially &mdash; new code</td><td>Trivially &mdash; new kernel</td><td>Possible (reload bitstream) but heavy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Energy efficiency"</td><td>Worst &mdash; lots of silicon never used</td><td>Better on its workload</td><td>Best &mdash; only the circuit you needed exists</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"Cost to get started"</td><td>You have one</td><td>You have one</td><td>$500 dev board &rarr; $40k data-center card</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The takeaway: an FPGA shines when you have <b>one workload</b> you'll run <b>millions of times</b> and you care about <b>energy and tail latency</b>. That's not a game loop &mdash; game logic branches too much. But it <em>is</em> an LLM doing matrix-multiply after matrix-multiply.</p>

<h2>3. How a C++ program "calls" an FPGA</h2>

<p>This is the part that maps cleanly onto what you've already built. From a C++ program's point of view, an FPGA is an <b>accelerator at the other end of a bus</b> &mdash; usually PCIe. You don't "call a function" on it the way you call <code class="inline">printf</code>. You move data into its memory, kick off a kernel, and wait for the result.</p>

<div class="chip-diagram">
   your C++ host           the bus            the FPGA card
   +---------------+      +--------+      +--------------------+
   | game / driver | ===> |  PCIe  | ===> |  bitstream is your |
   | OpenCL/XRT API|      | (or    |      |  custom circuit:   |
   |               | <=== |  HBM   | <=== |  matrix multiply,  |
   +---------------+      |  link) |      |  attention kernel, |
                          +--------+      |  whatever you need |
                                          +--------------------+
   data flow:
   1. host allocates a buffer (host RAM, mapped to FPGA card via OpenCL)
   2. host writes input data into it
   3. host enqueues the kernel  (a one-line API call)
   4. FPGA does the work (everything runs in parallel inside)
   5. host waits, then reads the output buffer</div>

<p>Here is the same pattern in C++ using AMD's XRT (the Xilinx Runtime, which lives behind the Vitis HLS toolchain). It looks deceptively like our <code class="inline">LlmClient</code>:</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;xrt/xrt_device.h&gt;</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;xrt/xrt_kernel.h&gt;</span>
<span class="pp">#include</span> <span class="str">&lt;xrt/xrt_bo.h&gt;</span>

<span class="typ">int</span> main() {
    <span class="com">// 1. Open the FPGA card and load the bitstream — analogous to</span>
    <span class="com">//    "ollama serve starting up with a model loaded".</span>
    <span class="kw">auto</span> device = xrt::device(<span class="num">0</span>);
    <span class="kw">auto</span> uuid   = device.load_xclbin(<span class="str">"matmul_kernel.xclbin"</span>);
    <span class="kw">auto</span> kernel = xrt::kernel(device, uuid, <span class="str">"matmul"</span>);

    <span class="com">// 2. Allocate device buffers and fill them — like building a JSON body.</span>
    <span class="kw">auto</span> A = xrt::bo(device, N*N*<span class="kw">sizeof</span>(<span class="typ">float</span>), kernel.group_id(<span class="num">0</span>));
    <span class="kw">auto</span> B = xrt::bo(device, N*N*<span class="kw">sizeof</span>(<span class="typ">float</span>), kernel.group_id(<span class="num">1</span>));
    <span class="kw">auto</span> C = xrt::bo(device, N*N*<span class="kw">sizeof</span>(<span class="typ">float</span>), kernel.group_id(<span class="num">2</span>));
    A.write(matrixA); A.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_TO_DEVICE);
    B.write(matrixB); B.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_TO_DEVICE);

    <span class="com">// 3. Launch the kernel — like cli.Post("/api/chat", ...) in LlmClient.cpp.</span>
    <span class="kw">auto</span> run = kernel(A, B, C, N);

    <span class="com">// 4. Wait for it (blocking) — or .start()/.wait() async like our worker thread does.</span>
    run.wait();

    <span class="com">// 5. Read result back to host RAM — like parsing res-&gt;body into reply.content.</span>
    C.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_FROM_DEVICE);
    C.read(resultMatrix);
}</pre>

<p>Notice the five-step shape: <b>set up &rarr; ship inputs &rarr; launch &rarr; wait &rarr; read outputs</b>. That's structurally identical to a network call to Ollama. The only difference is what's on the other side: instead of a process talking HTTP, it's a circuit talking memory.</p>

<h2>4. LLMs on FPGAs &mdash; the exciting part</h2>

<p>Until recently the idea of running a language model on an FPGA was a research curiosity. As of 2026 it's real, and the results are interesting enough that AMD and a couple of startups are pouring money into it.</p>

<div class="callout real">
  <h3>Real 2026 numbers</h3>
  <p>The <b>LUT-LLM</b> paper (Nov 2025) implemented Qwen 3 1.7B &mdash; not a toy &mdash; on an AMD Alveo V80 FPGA. On the generation step it reported <b>1.10&ndash;3.29&times; faster than a GPU</b>, and <b>3.05&ndash;6.60&times; better energy efficiency</b>. A Versal/Alveo system running Mamba2-2.7B hit ~93 tokens/s on the prefill, ~200 tokens/s in decode on an Alveo U280, and ~333 tokens/s in decode on a VHK158.</p>
</div>

<p>Why does an FPGA win on LLM inference at all? Three reasons:</p>

<ol>
  <li><b>The arithmetic is regular and quantized.</b> Modern LLM inference is mostly multiplying weight matrices by activation vectors at 4- or 8-bit precision. That's the workload where an FPGA's dedicated DSPs and customizable bit widths beat a GPU's fixed FP16/BF16 lanes.</li>
  <li><b>You can build the exact dataflow.</b> A GPU has fixed-size warps and a fixed cache hierarchy. An FPGA lets you wire one layer's output straight into the next layer's input <em>on chip</em>, without round-tripping through HBM. That's where the energy savings come from.</li>
  <li><b>Lookup-table tricks like LUT-LLM.</b> A 2025 insight: at very low bit-width, you can replace some multiplies with pre-baked table lookups in the FPGA's BRAM. CPUs and GPUs can't do that cheaply; FPGAs absolutely can &mdash; LUTs are their native unit.</li>
</ol>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Model</th><th>Throughput</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>AMD Alveo V80</td><td>Qwen 3 1.7B (LUT-LLM)</td><td>1.1&ndash;3.3&times; a GPU baseline</td><td>Generation, mid-2026 prototype</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Alveo U280</td><td>Mamba2-2.7B</td><td>93 tok/s prefill &middot; ~200 tok/s decode</td><td>Spatial accelerator</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Versal VCK190</td><td>Mamba2-2.7B</td><td>7.21 tok/s</td><td>Embedded class card</td></tr>
    <tr><td>VHK158</td><td>Mamba2-2.7B</td><td>~333 tok/s decode</td><td>HBM-equipped data-center card</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The catches, in plain English:</p>
<ul>
  <li>An Alveo V80 is several thousand dollars; a VHK158 is tens of thousands. For a hobby game, you'd use a GPU.</li>
  <li>"Programming" the FPGA means an HLS or HDL flow, a multi-hour synthesis run, and probably $1k+ of Vitis tooling (or its open-source approximations).</li>
  <li>You target <b>one model at a time</b>. Switching from Qwen 3 to Llama 3 means reloading a different bitstream &mdash; potentially recompiling it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>5. In your project (hypothetically)</h2>

<p>Your game doesn't have an FPGA in it. But here's the punchline: <b>your <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> wouldn't care if you added one</b>. The whole reason we made <code class="inline">processOne()</code> the only place that knows about Ollama is precisely so we could replace it later. Here's what would change &mdash; and what wouldn't.</p>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:67 — today</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="com">// Sidecar implementation, HTTP to Ollama.</span>
ChatReply LlmClient::processOne(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) {
    httplib::Client cli(config_.host, config_.port);
    <span class="kw">auto</span> res = cli.Post(<span class="str">"/api/chat"</span>, buildJson(req), <span class="str">"application/json"</span>);
    <span class="com">// ... parse and return ...</span>
}</pre>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:67 — hypothetical FPGA backend</small>
<pre class="code"><span class="com">// Same signature, different transport — XRT call to a Qwen kernel on Alveo.</span>
ChatReply LlmClient::processOne(<span class="kw">const</span> ChatRequest&amp; req) {
    <span class="kw">auto</span> tokens = tokenize(req);                            <span class="com">// CPU</span>
    inputBuf_.write(tokens.data());
    inputBuf_.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_TO_DEVICE);                <span class="com">// PCIe -&gt; FPGA</span>
    <span class="kw">auto</span> run = qwenKernel_(inputBuf_, outputBuf_, tokens.size());
    run.wait();                                              <span class="com">// FPGA does the work</span>
    outputBuf_.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_FROM_DEVICE);             <span class="com">// FPGA -&gt; PCIe</span>
    ChatReply reply;
    reply.id = req.id;
    reply.ok = <span class="kw">true</span>;
    reply.content = detokenize(outputBuf_);                  <span class="com">// CPU</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> reply;
}</pre>

<p>The function body changes completely. But nothing else in the codebase moves:</p>

<ul>
  <li><code class="inline">LlmClient::submit()</code>, <code class="inline">drainReplies()</code>, <code class="inline">workerLoop()</code> &mdash; unchanged.</li>
  <li><code class="inline">Npc</code>, <code class="inline">Persona</code>, history-trimming &mdash; unchanged.</li>
  <li><code class="inline">DialogUI</code>, <code class="inline">main.cpp</code> &mdash; unchanged.</li>
  <li>Your async drain pattern, your thread-safe queues &mdash; unchanged.</li>
</ul>

<p>That's the whole reward for the encapsulation we did in lesson 0001. From the game's point of view, "Wren's brain is on an FPGA" and "Wren's brain is in a Python process down the hall" are indistinguishable as long as <code class="inline">processOne()</code> turns a request into a reply.</p>

<h2>6. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3>FPGA vs. ASIC</h3>
<p>An <b>ASIC</b> (Application-Specific Integrated Circuit) is a chip designed and fabricated for one job &mdash; an NVIDIA H100 is an ASIC, your phone's neural engine is an ASIC. It's faster and more power-efficient than the same design on an FPGA, but the design is frozen in silicon. FPGA is the reprogrammable cousin: you give up some efficiency in exchange for being able to ship a new bitstream tomorrow. Most chips that ship to consumers in volume are ASICs; FPGAs dominate prototyping, niche workloads, and any case where the workload mutates faster than chip fab cycles (years).</p>

<h3>FPGA vs. GPU</h3>
<p>Both are massively parallel and live on PCIe. The difference is rigidity. A GPU's parallelism is fixed: 32-wide warps, fixed-size caches, a fixed instruction set. You write a kernel that maps your problem onto that grid. An FPGA's parallelism is whatever you wire up: 17-wide pipelines, 12-bit arithmetic, on-chip RAM blocks placed where you need them. GPUs are easier to program and have better tooling; FPGAs are more efficient when your workload is regular and you're willing to spend weeks tuning.</p>

<h3>FPGA vs. microcontroller</h3>
<p>A microcontroller (Arduino, STM32) is a tiny CPU. An FPGA can <em>contain</em> a microcontroller as one circuit among many &mdash; that's called a "soft core" &mdash; but the FPGA itself is fundamentally different: customizable wiring, not fixed CPU logic. People sometimes use FPGAs because they need <em>both</em> a tiny CPU and a hardware pipeline next to it.</p>

<h3>FPGA on a board vs. eFPGA inside an SoC</h3>
<p>Modern AMD Versal chips put a small FPGA fabric <em>inside</em> the same package as ARM CPU cores and a GPU. This is an <b>embedded FPGA (eFPGA)</b>. From a programmer's view it's still "configurable hardware you call from C++"; from a hardware view it lives on the same die as everything else, so the PCIe step disappears. The LLM-on-FPGA work increasingly targets Versal AI Edge SoCs for exactly this reason.</p>

<h2>7. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You "program" an FPGA. What does that actually mean at the chip level?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That describes a CPU. An FPGA has no instruction set in the conventional sense — there's no fetch/decode/execute cycle running your code.">
      You write machine instructions to a small instruction memory and the chip executes them in order.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. The bitstream programs which LUTs/FFs/DSPs are active and how they're wired. After loading, the chip *is* the circuit you described — until you load a different bitstream.">
      You load a bitstream that configures which logic blocks are active and how they're wired into a circuit.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="GPUs run kernels — many copies of one program across thousands of threads. An FPGA doesn't have threads at all.">
      You upload a kernel that runs across thousands of parallel threads on a fixed instruction set.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That happens once in the silicon fab, years before you buy the chip. FPGAs are programmable *after* fabrication — that's the point.">
      You permanently etch a custom design into the silicon during the fabrication process.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Why is an FPGA <em>not</em> a good fit for your game's main loop (the SFML/OpenGL update-and-render code in <code class="inline">cpp_shooter_game</code> or <code class="inline">cpp_racing_game</code>)?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="FPGAs absolutely can handle floating point — Versal DSPs do FP32 and FP16. That's not the disqualifier.">
      FPGAs can only do integer math, and game physics needs floats.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Game-loop code is full of branches that depend on player input — different code paths each frame. FPGAs shine on regular dataflow where the same operation repeats; they're terrible at branchy serial logic. A CPU is the right tool there.">
      Game-loop code is branchy and irregular — the FPGA strength is regular dataflow, not unpredictable control flow.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="SFML is a software library — it doesn't care what chip it runs on. The disqualifier is conceptual, not platform-specific.">
      SFML doesn't have FPGA bindings.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Capacity is plenty for game logic. The reason it's wrong is *type* of workload, not size.">
      An FPGA doesn't have enough memory to hold the game state.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">In the hypothetical FPGA backend for <code class="inline">LlmClient::processOne()</code>, the host C++ code calls <code class="inline">inputBuf_.sync(XCL_BO_SYNC_BO_TO_DEVICE)</code>. What is this doing, and why is it necessary?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no such thing in this pattern. xclbin is the bitstream file, loaded once at startup, not per-request.">
      Loading a fresh bitstream into the FPGA so it knows what model to run.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. The buffer's contents live in host RAM until you sync them; sync copies them over PCIe to the FPGA's local memory so the kernel can read them. Without it the FPGA would read stale or empty data.">
      Copying the buffer contents over PCIe from host RAM to the FPGA's on-card memory so the kernel can read them.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That'd be a programming error in xrt::bo construction, not a sync operation. The buffer is allocated by xrt::bo earlier.">
      Allocating a new buffer on the FPGA each time.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Mutexes live on the host. The PCIe transfer is the synchronisation; there's no shared-memory lock involved.">
      Acquiring a mutex so the worker thread doesn't read while the FPGA writes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Why do FPGAs win on energy efficiency for LLM inference (~3&ndash;6&times; a GPU in the LUT-LLM result)?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Same chemistry, very different transistor counts and densities. The win comes from *what gets used*, not from running cooler at the same job.">
      FPGAs run at a much lower temperature, so their transistors switch with less leakage.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Higher clocks usually cost more power per operation, not less. FPGAs typically clock *lower* than GPUs.">
      FPGAs clock higher than GPUs, so each operation takes fewer joules.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. A GPU runs at fixed FP16/BF16 lanes, fixed cache, fixed warp width — silicon you didn't need still draws power. An FPGA's bitstream only lights up exactly the circuit your workload uses, at exactly the bit-widths it uses, and pipes layer-to-layer data on-chip instead of round-tripping through HBM.">
      The bitstream only lights up the circuit the workload actually uses, at the bit-widths it actually uses, and routes data on-chip instead of through HBM.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Memory hierarchies are still part of the design — HBM is on many FPGA cards (e.g. VHK158, VCU128) precisely because LLM weights are big. The win isn't 'no memory', it's 'less unnecessary round-tripping'.">
      FPGAs don't use any memory hierarchy — the weights are baked into the gates.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You decide one day to swap Ollama for an FPGA-hosted Qwen kernel. Which of the following files will you have to change?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Persona and Npc have nothing to do with how inference happens. The whole point of the LlmClient abstraction is to keep those layers ignorant.">
      <code class="inline">Persona.hpp</code>, <code class="inline">Npc.cpp</code>, and <code class="inline">DialogUI.cpp</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="main.cpp constructs an LlmClient; it doesn't know about HTTP or PCIe. Swapping the backend doesn't change construction.">
      Mostly <code class="inline">main.cpp</code>, plus a new CMake target.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. processOne() is the only place that knows about the transport. Everything else — submit, drainReplies, the worker loop, the queues, the persona layer, the UI — keeps working unchanged. CMake needs to link XRT instead of vendored httplib.">
      <code class="inline">LlmClient::processOne()</code> in <code class="inline">LlmClient.cpp</code>, and the <code class="inline">CMakeLists.txt</code> to link XRT instead of httplib.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="You'd absolutely not need to rewrite from scratch — the whole layered design was built to make this change cheap. Skipping the abstraction was the cost-saving move at the start, not the rewrite later.">
      All of them &mdash; you'd rewrite the codebase against XRT from scratch.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

